# Copy to `.env` at the repo root. `.env` is gitignored -- never commit it.
#
# One file for the whole cohort: every notebook and every challenge app reads
# from here, via helpers.nb.bootstrap().
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# YOU BRING THE MODEL.
#
# There is no shared class server. You need EITHER an API key from a provider
# OR an endpoint you can reach -- your firm's internal gateway, a server you
# run, or a model on your own laptop.
#
# That is not us being unhelpful. It is the actual situation you will be in at
# work, and an application that treats its provider as configuration survives
# it. One that hardcodes a vendor SDK gets rewritten in Week 9.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# --- Option 1: a hosted provider, with your own key -------------------------
# LiteLLM picks the provider from the model string.

OPENAI_API_KEY=sk-...
LLM_MODEL=gpt-4.1-mini

# Anthropic
# ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=sk-ant-...
# LLM_MODEL=anthropic/claude-sonnet-5

# Azure OpenAI -- common inside large firms
# AZURE_API_KEY=...
# AZURE_API_BASE=https://your-resource.openai.azure.com
# AZURE_API_VERSION=2024-10-21
# LLM_MODEL=azure/your-deployment-name

# --- Option 2: an endpoint you can reach ------------------------------------
# Anything OpenAI-compatible: your firm's internal gateway, vLLM, LM Studio,
# llama.cpp, or a colleague's server. Prefix the model with `openai/` so
# LiteLLM uses the OpenAI-compatible path, and set the base URL.

# OPENAI_API_KEY=EMPTY
# LLM_MODEL=openai/your-model-name
# LLM_API_BASE=http://your-server:8000/v1
# LLM_EXTRA_HEADERS={"x-bf-vk": "sk-bf-..."}   # JSON; e.g. a Bifrost virtual key

# --- Option 3: a model on your own laptop -----------------------------------
# No key, no network, nothing leaves the machine. Slower, and worth doing at
# least once -- Week 2 is largely about why this option matters.

# LLM_MODEL=ollama/llama3.2
# LLM_API_BASE=http://localhost:11434

# --- Adversarial work: prompt injection and red-teaming (Week 6) -----------
# The guardrails week attacks YOUR OWN application -- hand-written injections,
# an optimiser that searches hundreds of variants, poisoned documents, poisoned
# tool descriptions. Those prompts must NEVER go to a public API by model name:
# every major provider's usage policy prohibits "circumventing safeguards" or
# "prompt injection" with no carve-out for testing your own app, and their
# enforcement is automated pattern-matching that cannot tell your system prompt
# from their safety training. Azure's Prompt Shields flag this exact content by
# default and feed abuse monitoring that can suspend a subscription -- i.e. your
# employer's. Following the class should never cost anyone an account.
#
# So the Week 6 notebooks and challenge read THESE keys, with no fallback to
# LLM_MODEL. Leave them unset and the notebooks halt at Setup and tell you why.
#
# Default: Ollama on your laptop. Needs a model that can call tools (Task 7).
#     ollama pull qwen2.5:7b          # best small-model tool calling
#     ollama pull qwen2.5:3b          # CPU-only laptop: faster, a little noisier
# The `ollama_chat/` prefix is what makes LiteLLM pass tool calls through.

# INJECTION_MODEL=ollama_chat/qwen2.5:7b
# INJECTION_API_BASE=http://localhost:11434

# Any other OpenAI-compatible local server works -- LM Studio, vLLM, llama.cpp:
# INJECTION_MODEL=openai/<the model id the server reports>
# INJECTION_API_BASE=http://localhost:1234/v1
# INJECTION_API_KEY=EMPTY

# Or a gateway your firm has APPROVED for adversarial testing (ask; do not assume):
# INJECTION_MODEL=openai/<deployment>
# INJECTION_API_BASE=https://your-gateway/v1
# INJECTION_API_KEY=...

# --- Embeddings (from Week 3 onward) ----------------------------------------
EMBED_MODEL=text-embedding-3-small
# EMBED_API_BASE=

# --- Vision (multimodal retrieval) ------------------------------------------
# A chat model that can accept images as well as text. There is no default and
# no fallback to LLM_MODEL on purpose: most chat models cannot see, and one that
# cannot will still accept an image payload and answer confidently about
# nothing. Better to be told it is missing.
#
# Anything OpenAI-compatible that supports `image_url` content parts works --
# a hosted multimodal model, or a local server with a vision-capable model
# loaded.

# VLM_MODEL=gpt-4.1-mini
# VLM_MODEL=openai/your-multimodal-model
# VLM_API_BASE=http://localhost:8888/v1

# A separate host usually means a separate credential. Set these only when the
# embedding or vision endpoint needs a different key from OPENAI_API_KEY above;
# both fall back to it when unset.
# EMBED_API_KEY=
# VLM_API_KEY=

# --- Behaviour --------------------------------------------------------------
SYSTEM_PROMPT=You are a helpful assistant. Be concise and concrete.
